{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XB3SFXoPM79dmgUa5ZaXKg","type":"tool_result","content":"406:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** etcd is where the cluster lives. It is the backing store for *all* cluster data [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23], which means the other components can be killed, restarted, or replaced and rebuild their working picture from what etcd holds. etcd itself holds the thing that cannot be rebuilt from anywhere else. Losing it is not something you recover from by restarting a process. It's something you recover from by having taken a backup, which is why the documentation's one piece of unsolicited advice about etcd is \"make sure you have a back up plan.\"\n408:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 — etcd backup and restore in practice]*\n1294:| **etcd** | Consistent, highly-available key value store. Backing store for *all* cluster data. Access to it is root-equivalent. Have a backup plan. |\n=== Ch2 headings ===\n130:## *\"Why the shipping container beat the ship\"*\n136:## Attention Budget\n170:## 🧭 Soundings\n239:## Why This Chapter Matters\n251:## What You'll Learn\n267:## §1 — ⚪ What a Container Actually Is\n322:## §2 — ⚪ What's Inside an Image\n399:## §3 — 🔵 Registries, Tags, and Digests\n463:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1: Containers, Images, and Identity\n533:## §4 — 🔵 The Container Runtime Interface\n608:## §5 — 🔵 The Open Container Initiative\n675:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2: Who Runs It, and Who Standardized It\n743:## §6 — 🟡 When Kubernetes Pulls, and When It Doesn't\n787:## §7 — 🟡 Not All Isolation Is Equal: RuntimeClass\n817:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3: Pull Behavior and Runtime Selection\n885:## §8 — 🔵 The Crate, Not the Cargo\n918:## Exam Alert 🚨\n949:## Practice Questions\n1285:## Chapter Summary\n1316:## The Voyage Ahead","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a14c5a2b-d3b1-4d5e-aa66-b9d0f14e40bb","uuid":"9a744de9-d553-4233-8ead-0ccb7c50bf78","timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:4