{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KeKGcXfUmoVfvMxLzXJ8Ee","type":"tool_result","content":"100:The shape is the more interesting thing, though. You have been typing commands in exactly this form since Chapter 4 (`kubectl apply -f`, `kubectl get pods`, `kubectl scale`) and nobody has told you what the form *is*. It worked anyway. That is precisely the condition under which a candidate walks into the exam room confident and then loses five points to a question about which component is permitted to be one minor version newer than which.\n129:`kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml`. `kubectl get pods`. `kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=5`. Every one of them worked. Nobody told you what the shape was, and you did not need to be told, which is exactly why it earns ten minutes now.\n146:  kubectl   get              pods\n157:**Figure 8.1 —** Five commands you already know, aligned on the same four slots. What to notice is the empty columns: `kubectl get pods` omits NAME and flags, and gets every Pod as a result. What to notice second is the asymmetry at the bottom, which is the examinable half of this section.\n203:> 🪝 **Snag:** `kubectl` inside a Pod does not use your kubeconfig, is not you, and does not default to the `default` namespace. It authenticates as the Pod's ServiceAccount and looks in that ServiceAccount's namespace. Every practitioner who has ever run a debugging shell inside a cluster has been surprised by this at least once, usually by an empty `kubectl get pods` that they were certain should have returned something.\n374:[Omitted long matching line]\n400:**2.** ⚪ You run `kubectl get pods` from a shell on your laptop, and again from inside a running Pod in the same cluster. Both succeed and return different results. Explain *both* differences — what identity each invocation used, and what namespace each looked in.\n531:[Omitted long matching line]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a14c5a2b-d3b1-4d5e-aa66-b9d0f14e40bb","uuid":"e2efe7db-2e99-4212-bfe3-29d9446e3168","timestamp":"2026-08-31T03:47:28.324Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"100:The shape is the more interesting thing, though. You have been typing commands in exactly this form since Chapter 4 (`kubectl apply -f`, `kubectl get pods`, `kubectl scale`) and nobody has told you what the form *is*. It worked anyway. That is precisely the condition under which a candidate walks into the exam room confident and then loses five points to a question about which component is permitted to be one minor version newer than which.\n129:`kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml`. `kubectl get pods`. `kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=5`. Every one of them worked. Nobody told you what the shape was, and you did not need to be told, which is exactly why it earns ten minutes now.\n146:  kubectl   get              pods\n157:**Figure 8.1 —** Five commands you already know, aligned on the same four slots. What to notice is the empty columns: `kubectl get pods` omits NAME and flags, and gets every Pod as a result. What to notice second is the asymmetry at the bottom, which is the examinable half of this section.\n203:> 🪝 **Snag:** `kubectl` inside a Pod does not use your kubeconfig, is not you, and does not default to the `default` namespace. It authenticates as the Pod's ServiceAccount and looks in that ServiceAccount's namespace. Every practitioner who has ever run a debugging shell inside a cluster has been surprised by this at least once, usually by an empty `kubectl get pods` that they were certain should have returned something.\n374:[Omitted long matching line]\n400:**2.** ⚪ You run `kubectl get pods` from a shell on your laptop, and again from inside a running Pod in the same cluster. Both succeed and return different results. Explain *both* differences — what identity each invocation used, and what namespace each looked in.\n531:[Omitted long matching line]","numLines":8,"totalLines